  "notes": [
    "EMBED — menuCategories and items inside Restaurant: Menu data is always read alongside the restaurant document for browsing and checkout; menu mutations (add/edit/remove item) are infrequent manager operations that do not contend with customer reads. This avoids a separate collection join on the most traffic-heavy browse path and keeps the document bounded because a restaurant's menu is finite.",
    "EMBED — savedAddresses inside User: Addresses belong exclusively to a single customer and are always fetched with the user profile during checkout. They have no independent identity across aggregates.",
    "EMBED — orderItems (with price snapshot) inside Order: REQ-23 and dispute resolution require preserving the price that was charged at order time. Embedding immutably snapshots name and unitPrice at placement, decoupling the order from future menu price changes.",
    "EMBED — deliveryAddress snapshot inside Order: Captures the exact address the order was dispatched to; changes to a user's savedAddresses after placement must not affect the historical record.",
    "EMBED — statusHistory inside Order: Status transitions are an intrinsic part of the order lifecycle, are always read together for tracking, and are append-only, making embedding natural.",
    "EMBED — refunds inside Payment: Refunds belong exclusively to a payment, are read together for receipts and dispute resolution, and their count per order is small (typically 0 or 1).",
    "REFERENCE — Payment from Order (paymentId): Payment has its own write lifecycle (gateway webhooks, capture events, refunds) driven by async gateway callbacks. A separate collection allows those writes to scale independently without contending on the Order document. The Order carries a paymentId backref for quick lookup.",
    "REFERENCE — Delivery from Order (deliveryId): Agent location updates arrive at high frequency over WebSocket/polling and must not lock the Order document. A dedicated Deliveries collection allows the real-time tracking service to update agentLiveLocation without any impact on order status reads. The Order carries a deliveryId backref.",
    "REFERENCE — Review from Order (reviewId): Reviews are written once, read infrequently relative to orders, and need admin moderation actions. A separate collection keeps the Order document lean and allows index-efficient admin queries across all reviews.",
    "REFERENCE — managerId / agentId as ObjectId refs to User: Managers and agents are independent account entities with their own auth and profile lifecycle; they are not owned by Restaurant or Delivery.",
    "DENORMALISED — avgRating and reviewCount on Restaurant: These fields are read on every browse result card (REQ-11) and are updated only on new review or review removal; a post-save hook on Review recomputes and persists them, avoiding a costly aggregation pipeline on each browse request.",
    "Cart TTL index on lastActivityAt (72 hours / 259200 s): Aligns with the glossary definition of Cart as a transient object and avoids stale carts accumulating. The TTL should be made configurable via PlatformConfig in production.",
    "GeoJSON Point fields (address.location on Restaurant, currentLocation on User.agentProfile, restaurantLocation/deliveryLocation/agentLiveLocation on Delivery, savedAddresses[].location on User) all carry 2dsphere indexes to support the proximity-based agent assignment (REQ-25) and restaurant-radius filtering (BR-4).",
    "PlatformConfig uses a key/value Mixed design to store commission rate, service fee, default delivery radius, lockout duration, and other admin-configurable parameters (REQ-42) without requiring schema migrations for new parameters.",
    "AdminAuditLog is intentionally append-only with no Mongoose timestamps update path; occurredAt is set at write time by the application to ensure immutability of the audit trail.",
    "No raw card data fields exist anywhere in the schema (PCI DSS compliance); only gatewayTransactionRef and gatewayPaymentToken (an opaque reference returned by the gateway) are stored on Payment.",
    "The agentProfile and managerProfile subdocuments on User are sparse — they are only populated for the relevant role. This avoids creating separate Agent and Manager collections for what is essentially a User with role-specific attributes, keeping authentication and account management in one collection.",
    "Human should confirm: (1) whether proof-of-delivery photos are stored as URLs to an object-store bucket (assumed here) or as Buffer blobs; (2) whether a separate Disputes collection is needed for REQ-41 or whether adminAuditLogs plus order status transitions are sufficient; (3) the desired cart TTL duration; (4) whether multi-restaurant manager accounts are required (currently one restaurantId per managerProfile)."
  ],
  "datastore_product": "MongoDB"
}